 it all-time  highs the stock market is doing well  across the board as a matter of fact you  know things are the the economy is  growing not it's not doing great but at  least it's moving again but for average  people it's as though the recession  never ended we've got this kind of  curious situation that we're in now  where something fundamental has changed  about American life and Obama himself  has talked about it right he's the one  that says that inequality is the great  issue of our time Obama himself talks  about it very eloquently but he hasn't  been able to do anything about it or he  hasn't tried to do he's failed to do  what basically essentially what needed  to be done and that's the starting point  of the book I wonder why that is so  let's see if we can break down some of  these ideas into component parts I mean  I think there's no question whatsoever  that any self-described progressive  would look at the totality of the Obama  administration and say I can find  victories but I also find a by-and-large  continuation of the broader status quo  and this is sort of related to the the  narrow scope of what even counts as  valid debate in politics in the United  States is it slightly left of center or  is it to the right of the center but I  would couch it sort of in a different  way and I'm curious what your reaction  is and I know that in the book you  reject the idea that President Obama's  lack of more progressivism can be  attributed to Republican obstructionism  I know that that's not an idea you find  particularly valid or you or your  support but my question is this which is  given the pace and at which we know the  American government moves and get things  gets things done and given the reality  that Republican obstructionism or the  brought the broader huge American  apparatus of government that exists  can't we say that given what one  president could do in  years there's a fair number of  accomplishments that that even someone  like for example Ted Kennedy who had an  incrementalist approach would say we've  taken a lot of steps to the left well so  no I think that he could have done a lot  more and I think that the the country  would have been behind him and doing a  lot more the country was certainly  behind him in 2008  you remember those enormous rallies yeah  and this this was a guy who had the wind  at his back and he had majorities in  both houses of Congress and he had these  you know Chicago tough guys like rahm  emanuel with him who knew how the system  worked and knew how to twist arms and  you know and he came away with given  that he came away with quite little in  my view now so i don't downplay  republican obstructionism i wrote a  whole book about republican  obstructionism called the wrecking crew  a couple of years ago i mean this is  this is you know this is an important  fact of life in washington DC what's  fascinating to me is that when Barack  Obama came into office he pretended he  had never heard of this the you know the  problem of Republican obstructions like  this is a surprise to him you know he  when he reached out to people like Chuck  Grassley you know on Obama care and let  this guy basically sabotage the you know  the workings OB you remember this way  when I dragged on for weeks and weeks  and weeks while he waited for chuck  Grassley to sign off on some part of it  so that he could claim it was bipartisan  it's like he had missed all of the 1990s  you know that when Newt Gingrich made  obstructionism basically the philosophy  of the Republican Party and so that was  um you know I know that being bipartisan  is supposed to be the last word in  sophistication and pragmatism but this  struck me as as really naive you know  especially SiC given who you know that  Obama could easily have you know turn to  any of the people around him including  someone like Bill Clinton who knew about  this too firsthand and you know Bill  Clinton who's not a man that I admire  but but plenty of people and all I'm  saying is lots of people in Washington  know about Republican obstruction  it should not have been a surprise to  him coming into office in 2009 though he  should have mino' there there were all  sorts of ways to deal with it for one  thing he had majorities in both houses  with a simple sort of structural  adjustment in the Senate even the you  know the filibuster wouldn't matter  anymore something that I think by the  way is long overdue they should have  done away with the the power of you know  of a minority of senators to stop  legislation that should have been done a  long time ago Obama was a senator he  knew this you know that that he then  gave Republicans essentially handed them  veto power over his own program when he  didn't need to when there's no  structural reason to do it you know  that's his mistake that's not you know  that's not the Republicans per se we  know what they're gonna do right that we  always know they're always do the same  thing we know what they're gonna do  that's his mistake  that's his blunder and you know or is it  a blunder  that's what I started wondering after a  while I mean you you know when they when  you look at something like the way that  Obama dealt with the banks where it's  not you know the tarp was already passed  he had the power of of you know of the  US Treasury they just bailed out all the  you know the big banks on Wall Street so  many of the smaller regional banks in  this country he had a complete power  over these institutions he could have  done whatever he wanted with them he had  seats on their board for pete's sake you  know he can't his votes within the banks  or he did at the time it's all gone now  but he could have fired the management  as he did with General Motors he could  have you know forced them to divest lots  of their parts of their operation he  could have shut certain banks down put  them into receivership all of these  things were totally within his power and  he chose not to do them and that's the  critical thing when you look at the  Obama administration and by the way I  confess to being guilty on this I want  to believe the best about Barack Obama  as a man that I admired enormous ly at  one time and then I admire still I saw  him at the Democratic convention and  every time he speaks I'm just like  I'm blown away by this guy I think he's  the greatest okay at the same time we do  everything in our sort of critical power  to let him off the hook for what he did  as president and I don't think that's  responsible I don't think that's that's  right you know that's responsible yeah  if we're going to be critics if we're  going to write about politics we have to  take ideas seriously we have to take  history seriously and we have to take  what what the president does and says  seriously and I think that the only  thing that you can conclude what you  have to conclude looking at the Obama  administration is that he did what he  did not because his hands were forced by  this you know power or that power this  inability or that inability but because  he chose to do things the way he did  well I think that that gets to and and  and we I'd had a couple other things I  wanted to broach and we do have limited  time left  I think this oh I'm sorry I talk way too  much I apologize for that this area's I  sort of wanted to get to which is in in  reading your book I get the more general  sense that your critique of the  Democratic Party is at least partially  based in the reality that the Democratic  Party while more to the left than the  Republican Party in the u.s. is still  fundamentally a pro-capitalist party it  is that underlying some of the critique  that you're you're outlining well yes of  course but it's not just that they're  pro-capitalist I mean everyone is that  you know it's that they're Pro a certain  kind of capitalism okay so let's never  forget when we talk about the kind of  whatever you want to call it  turbocharged capitalism or neoliberalism  or basically the deregulated modern free  trade era that we're living in that you  know we like to go back and blame it on  Ronald Reagan and that is I think fair  Ronald Reagan is really and Margaret  Thatcher is really where this modern era  of ours begins but the man who put this  agenda into effect more than anybody any  other president more than any other  figure is Bill Clinton a Democrat okay  this is the man who you know President  Reagan tried to overturn glass-steagall  you know he chipped away at it but it's  Bill Clinton that got the job done if  you look at Bill Clinton's presidency  all of these deregulations  the you know the deregulation of the  banking industry that's Clinton that did  that not Reagan and not Bush you know  this is Clinton that achieved those  things that his his staffers would boast  about that and it wasn't just one thing  it wasn't just overturning  glass-steagall there's a whole series of  bank D regulations while Clinton was  president the free trade deals  Republicans yes those are that NAFTA was  their idea it was a Republican idea but  it took a Democrat to get that passed it  was Bill Clinton that got that through  Congress and that was Bill Clinton then  who proposed you know all of the free  trade deals after that and then you look  at chipping away at the welfare state  Reagan tried but he couldn't get very  far  it was Clinton that did away with the  welfare system in this country AFDC that  was you know a New Deal program that was  Clinton that did away with that not  Reagan and in some ways it's only  possible for a Democrat to do these  things but if you when you talk about  the grand sweep of neoliberalism in this  country he is the central figure we have  to acknowledge that among ourselves and  the question becomes why why did  Democrats you know why did the party of  Franklin Roosevelt why are they so keen  to sign on you know sign on to this  agenda and that's the larger question  t